course: dictionary
lesson: 1193
title: Brushwork in art
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Brushwork is a word you will meet in art lessons. It means the way a painter uses a paintbrush, and the marks the brush leaves on the painting. Read the words around it to find the clues. «Loose brushwork, holding the brush further from the bristles, often makes paintings look more alive.» Loose tells you the brushwork is free and relaxed, not neat and tight.
explain: The sentence also tells you how the brush is held. Holding it further from the bristles changes the marks it makes. Alive suggests a picture full of movement and energy, not stiff or still. Try your meaning in the sentence: «Relaxed, free brush marks often make paintings look more alive» still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
explain: Artists describe brushwork to talk about their style. Thick, heavy strokes, tiny dots, smooth blending and quick dabs are all kinds of brushwork, and each one changes how a picture looks. Brushwork shows the painter's skill and choices, just as a writer's word choice shows their style.
fact: Brushwork means the way a painter uses a paintbrush, and the marks the brush leaves on a painting.
fact: Brushwork is uncountable, so you say some brushwork, not a brushwork.
fact: «Loose brushwork» means relaxed, free brush marks rather than neat, tight ones.
fact: «Bold brushwork» means strong, clear brush marks that you can see easily.
fact: Artists such as Van Gogh used thick, swirly brushwork, while Monet and the Impressionists used tiny dots, smooth blending and quick dabs.
exercise: What does brushwork mean? | The name of a shop that sells paint | The wooden frame around a picture | *The way a painter uses a paintbrush, and the marks the brush leaves | A type of paper for drawing
exercise: «Loose brushwork often makes paintings look more alive.» What does loose suggest here? | Wet and dripping | Very dark | Tight and neat | *Relaxed and free
exercise: Ella says brushwork is a finished painting. Is she right? | Yes, it means a finished picture hanging on a wall | Yes, it means the price of a painting | *No, it is the way a painter uses a brush and the marks it leaves | No, it is only the frame around a picture
test: «Loose brushwork, holding the brush further from the bristles, often makes paintings look more alive.» What does alive suggest? | *Full of energy and movement | Very small | Dull and still | Dead and cold
test: Your art teacher says, «Look closely at the brushwork in this painting.» What should you look at? | The price written on the label | The size of the frame | *The marks the brush left and how the paint was put on | The name of the gallery
exam: «Bold brushwork can be seen from across the room.» What does bold brushwork mean? | Shy, faint marks that are hard to see | *Strong, clear brush marks you can see easily | Brushwork that has been cut off | Brushwork done with a pen
